menuconfig ESP32C3_BT
    bool "Espressif ESP32-C3 Bluetooth LE controller"
    depends on SOC_ESP32C3
    select CRC
    select MCU
    select RNG
    help
      Link Espressif's Bluetooth LE controller and PHY libraries, from
      the esp32c3-bt-lib and esp-phy-lib submodules, with the glue that
      runs them on PebbleOS. Nothing starts the controller unless asked
      to: the btc shell command, or NimBLE's HCI transport once armed
      (or at every boot, with ESP32C3_BT_ARMED_BY_DEFAULT).
      Each bring-up arms the RTC watchdog until btc confirm proves the
      USB console alive, and a bring-up that never completed keeps the
      controller off at the following boots.

if ESP32C3_BT

config ESP32C3_BT_CONTROLLER_IN_FLASH
    bool "Controller linked whole in flash"
    help
      Link the controller library built to run from flash
      (libbtdm_app_flash.a) rather than the one that patches the
      controller in ROM (libbtdm_app.a). It saves about 23 KiB of RAM
      code, but its interrupt handlers then run from flash through the
      cache: on the Xteink X4 an advertising event's handler took over
      3 ms and the events were scheduled too late to go on air.

config ESP32C3_BT_CONTROLLER_PRIO
    int "Controller thread priority"
    default 4
    help
      Priority of the controller's thread, above the NimBLE host's, as
      ESP-IDF runs it two below its highest.

config ESP32C3_BT_CONTROLLER_STACK_SIZE
    int "Controller thread stack size in bytes"
    default 3584
    help
      ESP-IDF gives the controller task 3584 bytes.

config ESP32C3_BT_BLE_MAX_ACT
    int "BLE activities"
    range 1 10
    default 3
    help
      Advertising, scanning, connection and initiating instances the
      controller allocates for: each costs heap. The watch advertises
      and keeps one connection.

config ESP32C3_BT_CONFIRM_TIMEOUT_S
    int "Seconds to confirm a bring-up"
    range 5 3600
    default 30
    help
      The RTC watchdog resets the chip this long after a bring-up starts
      unless btc confirm stops it.

config ESP32C3_BT_ARMED_BY_DEFAULT
    bool "Use the controller without btc arm"
    help
      NimBLE's HCI transport brings the controller up at every boot, not
      only once btc arm asked for it. A bring-up that failed or hung
      still keeps the controller off at the following boots, on the fake
      HCI, until btc clear.

config ESP32C3_BT_REQUIRE_CONFIRM
    bool "Require a confirmation of every bring-up"
    default n if ESP32C3_BT_ARMED_BY_DEFAULT
    default y
    help
      Keep the RTC watchdog armed after a bring-up until btc confirm,
      typed on the console, proves the USB console alive; without it,
      the chip resets. Turn it off once the radio is proven on a board,
      e.g. for test runs that restart the watch: the watchdog then only
      covers the bring-up itself.

config ESP32C3_BT_SHELL
    bool "Shell commands"
    default y
    depends on SHELL
    help
      The btc command: start, confirm, arm and clear the controller,
      send it raw HCI packets, dump clock and power registers.

module = ESP32C3_BT
module-str = ESP32-C3 Bluetooth controller
source "subsys/logging/Kconfig.template.log_level"

endif # ESP32C3_BT
